WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:05.310
If you suspect your neural network is overfitting your data, that is, if you have a high variance

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:09.430
problem, one of the first things to try is probably regularization.

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:12.670
The other way to address high variance is to get more training data.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:13.670
There's also quite reliable.

00:00:13.670 --> 00:00:15.710
You can't always get more training data.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:20.950
It could be expensive to get more data, but adding regularization will often help to prevent

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:23.950
overfitting or to reduce variance in your neural network.

00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:26.110
So let's see how regularization works.

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:28.750
Let's develop these ideas using logistic regression.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:34.070
Recall that for logistic regression, you try to minimize the cost function j, which is defined

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:37.110
as this cost function.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:41.550
Some of your training examples of the losses of the individual predictions and the different

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:48.310
examples where you recall that w and b in logistic regression are the parameters.

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:54.670
So w is an x-dimensional parameter vector and b is a real number.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:01:00.630
And so to add regularization to logistic regression, what you do is add to it this thing, lambda,

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:03.310
which is called the regularization parameter.

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:04.310
Say more about that in a second.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:10.030
But lambda over 2m times the norm of w squared.

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:20.950
So here, the norm of w squared is just equal to sum from j equals 1 to nx of wj squared.

00:01:20.950 --> 00:01:26.190
This can also be written w transpose w is just a squared Euclidean norm of the parameter

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:27.750
vector w.

00:01:27.750 --> 00:01:35.910
And this is called L2 regularization because here you're using the Euclidean norm, also

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:38.710
it's called the L2 norm of the parameter vector w.

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:41.750
Now, why do you regularize just the parameter w?

00:01:41.750 --> 00:01:47.110
Why don't we add something here about b as well?

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:52.870
In practice, you could do this, but I usually just omit this because if you look at the

00:01:52.870 --> 00:01:59.510
parameters, w is usually a pretty high dimensional parameter vector, especially if a high variance

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:03.390
problem, maybe w just has a lot of parameters, so you aren't fitting all the parameters as

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:06.630
well, whereas b is just a single number.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:10.390
So almost all the parameters are in w rather than b.

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:14.110
And if you add this last term in practice, it won't make much of a difference because

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:17.630
b is just one parameter out of a very large number of parameters.

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:23.070
In practice, I usually just don't bother to include it, but you can if you want.

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:27.550
So L2 regularization is the most common type of regularization.

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:32.510
You might have also heard of some people talk about L1 regularization, and that's

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:41.190
when you add, instead of this L2 norm, you instead add a term that is lambda over m of

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:45.630
sum over of this.

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:51.630
And this is also called the L1 norm of the parameter vector w, so a little subscript 1

00:02:51.630 --> 00:02:52.630
down there.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:58.070
And I guess whether you put m or 2m in the denominator is just a scaling constant.

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:03.070
If you use L1 regularization, then w will end up being sparse.

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:08.150
And what that means is that the w vector will have a lot of zeros in it.

00:03:08.150 --> 00:03:11.870
And some people say that this can help with compressing the model because if some of the

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:15.910
parameters are zero, then you need less memory to store the model.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:19.710
Although I find that in practice, L1 regularization to make your model sparse

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:20.910
helps only a little bit.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:25.070
So I don't think it's used that much, at least not for the purpose of compressing your

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:26.070
model.

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:30.910
And when people train your networks, L2 regularization is just used much, much more often.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:34.430
Oh, sorry, it's fixing up some of the notation here.

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:45.460
So one last detail, lambda here is called the regularization parameter.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:50.060
And usually you set this using your development set or using holdout cross validation, where

00:03:50.060 --> 00:03:54.700
you try a variety of values and see what does the best in terms of trading off between

00:03:54.700 --> 00:04:00.060
doing well on your training set versus also setting the two norm of your parameters

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:03.160
to be small, which helps prevent overfitting.

00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:07.220
So lambda is another hyperparameter that you might have to tune.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:12.420
And by the way, for the programming exercises, lambda is a reserved keyword in the Python

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.300
programming language.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:21.380
So in the programming exercise, we'll have L-A-M-B-D without the A, so as not to clash

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:23.660
with a reserved keyword in Python.

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:29.180
So we'll use L-A-M-B-D to represent the lambda regularization parameter.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:33.300
So this is how you implement L2 regularization for logistic regression.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.300
How about a neural network?

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.380
In a neural network, you have a cost function.

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:45.540
There's a function of all of your parameters, w1, b1 through w capital L, b capital L, where

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.060
capital L is the number of layers in your neural network.

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:58.260
And so the cost function is this, sum of the losses, sum over your M training samples.

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:08.340
And so to add regularization, you add lambda over 2M of sum over all of your parameters,

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:17.580
w, your parameter matrix is w, of their, let's call it the squared norm, where this norm

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:25.020
of a matrix, really the squared norm, is defined as the sum of i, sum over j of each of the

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:29.260
elements of that matrix squared.

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:34.020
And if you want the indices of the summation, this is sum from i equals 1 through nL minus

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:45.100
1, sum from j equals 1 through nL, because w is a nL by nL minus 1 dimensional matrix,

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:49.620
where these are the number of hidden units in, or number of units in layers L minus 1

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:51.460
and layer L.

00:05:51.460 --> 00:06:00.940
So this matrix norm, it turns out, is called the Frobenius norm of a matrix denoted with

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:03.700
f in the subscript.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:09.860
So for our arcane linear algebra technical reasons, this is not called the L2 norm of

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:14.420
a matrix, instead it's called the Frobenius norm of a matrix.

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:18.220
I know it sounds like it would be more natural just call the L2 norm of the matrix, but

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:23.020
really arcane reasons that you don't need to know by convention, this is called the

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:27.380
Frobenius norm, which just means the sum of squared of elements of a matrix.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:30.420
So how do you implement gradient descent with this?

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:38.750
Previously, we would compute dW, you know, using backprop, where backprop would give

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:46.710
us the partial derivative of j with respect to W, or really W for any given L. And then

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:52.750
you update WL as WL minus the learning rate times d.

00:06:52.750 --> 00:06:57.950
So this is before we added this extra regularization term to the objective.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:02.110
Now that we've added this regularization term to the objective, what you do is you take

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:09.670
dW and you add to it lambda over m times W, and then you just compute this update

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:10.670
same as before.

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:16.670
And it turns out that with this new definition of dWL, this is still, you know,

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:23.550
this new dWL is still a correct definition of the derivative of your cost function with

00:07:23.550 --> 00:07:25.230
respect to your parameters.

00:07:25.230 --> 00:07:29.390
Now you've added the extra regularization term at the end.

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:36.750
And it's for this reason that L2 regularization is sometimes also called weight decay.

00:07:36.750 --> 00:07:43.830
So if I take this definition of dWL and just plug it in here, then you see that the

00:07:43.830 --> 00:07:50.710
update is WL gets updated as WL times the learning rate alpha times, you know, the

00:07:50.710 --> 00:08:02.750
thing from backprop plus lambda over m times WL.

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:04.390
So with a minus sign there.

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:15.230
And so this is equal to WL minus alpha lambda over m times WL minus alpha times,

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:18.780
you know, the thing you got from backprop.

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:23.980
And so this term shows that whatever the matrix WL is, you're going to make it,

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:25.380
you know, a little bit smaller, right?

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:30.220
This is actually as if you're taking the matrix W and you're multiplying it by 1

00:08:30.220 --> 00:08:32.860
minus alpha lambda over m.

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:37.740
You're really taking the matrix W and subtracting alpha lambda over m times.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:41.740
And so it's like you're multiplying the matrix W by this number, which is going to

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:43.500
be a little bit less than 1.

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:49.540
So this is why L2 norm regularization is also called weight decay, because it's

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:55.780
just like the ordinary gradient descent where you update W by subtracting alpha

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:59.140
times the original gradient got from backprop.

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:06.660
But now you're also, you know, multiplying W by this, by this thing, which is a little

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:08.260
bit less than 1.

00:09:08.260 --> 00:09:11.660
So the alternative name for L2 regularization is weight decay.

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:16.300
I'm not really going to use that name, but the intuition for why it's called

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:20.860
weight decay is that this first term here is equal to this.

00:09:20.860 --> 00:09:25.460
So you're just multiplying the weight matrix by a number slightly less than 1.

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:29.380
So that's how you implement L2 regularization in the neural network.

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:33.380
Now, one question that people sometimes ask me is, you know, hey, Andrew, why does

00:09:33.380 --> 00:09:35.540
regularization prevent overfitting?

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:39.340
Let's take a quick look at the next video and gain some intuition for how

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:41.780
regularization prevents overfitting.
